zΔ⚡b|=2df|+%3D%3D History %3D%3D|=1|+%0AThe |=4|-1|+ utility was developed in the early 1970s on the [[Unix]] operating system which was emerging from [[AT&T]] [[Bell Labs]] in Murray Hill, New Jersey.%0AThe final version, first shipped with the 5th Edition of Unix in 1974, was entirely written by [[Douglas McIlroy]].%0AThis research was published in a 1976 paper co%2Dwritten with [[James W. Hunt]] who developed an initial prototype of diff.%0A%0AMcIlroy's work was preceded and influenced by [[Stephen C. Johnson%7CSteve Johnson]]'s comparison program on [[GECOS]] and [[Mike Lesk]]'s '''proof''' program.%0A'''Proof''' originated on Unix and produced line%2Dby%2Dline changes like diff and even used angle%2Dbrackets (">" and "<") for presenting line insertions and deletions in the program's output.%0AThe [[heuristic]]s used in these early applications were, however, |=1|+eemed unrel|=1|+able.%0AThe |=1|+otential|=1|+use|=2|+lness of a diff tool provoked M|=1|+Ilroy into researching and designing a more robust tool that could be used in a variety of tas|=1|+s but perform well in the processing and space limitations of the [[PDP%2D11]]'s hardware.%0AHis approach resulted from collaboration also with individuals at Bell Labs including [[Alfred Aho]], [[Elliot Pinson]], [[Jeffrey Ullman]], and [[Harold S. Stone]].%0A%0AIn the context of Unix, the use of the [[Ed (Unix)%7Ced]] line editor provided diff with the natural ability to create machine%2Dusable "edit scripts".%0AThese edit scripts, when saved to a file, can, along with the original file, be reconstituted by '''ed''' into the modified file in its entirety.%0AThis greatly reduced the space necessary to maintain multiple versions of a file.%0AMcIlroy considered writing a post%2Dprocessor for diff where a variety of output formats could be designed and implemented, but he found it more frugal and simpler to have diff be responsible for generating the syntax and reverse%2Dorder input accepted by the '''ed''' command. In 1985, [[Larry Wall]] composed a separate utility, [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]], that generalized and extended the ability to modify files with diff output.%0A%0AIn diff's early years, common uses included comparing changes in the source of software code and markup for technical documents, verifying program debugging output, comparing filesystem listings and analyzing computer assembly code.%0AThe output targeted for '''ed''' was motivated to provide compression for a sequence of modifications made to a file.%0AThe [[SCCS%7CSource Code Control System]] (SCCS) emerged in the late 1970s as a direct consequence of this development.%0A%0AA conceptual predecessor of diff includes [[Project Xanadu]], a hypertext project established in 1960 that had envisioned a version tracking system necessary for its "transpointing windows" feature.%0AAs part of this feature, file differences were subsumed in the expansive term "[[transclusion]]", when a document has included in it parts of other documents or revisions.%0A%0AIn the digital realm of the humanities, computer comparison systems were understood to have been created for working on literary works published as large volumes.|=3b1f|
